cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, dataset, or paper, please cite as below."
title: "Geometry of Confabulation and Refusal in Small Language Models"
abstract: >-
  Faced with an unanswerable question, small language models can either refuse or
  confabulate: confidently produce a wrong answer. Can the hidden state before the
  first generated token provide a clue? Can we flip confabulations into refusals? I
  introduce ConfabQA, a 784-item benchmark whose obscure-pre-cutoff category breaks
  the typical correctness/knowledge-cutoff confound and map the geometry of the hidden
  state with linear probes, a sparse autoencoder (SAE), and direct optimization of
  intervention directions. On Qwen3-1.7B and Gemma 2 2B the probe separates upcoming
  refusals from confabulations well beyond prompt features (+7.4/+16.3 pp); on Llama
  3.2 3B the ConfabQA margin is within fold-to-fold spread, but a direct refusal probe
  on PopQA and TriviaQA recovers +18-25 pp. On Qwen I closely inspect three candidate
  directions for the refusal vector: probe-recovered, Qwen-Scope SAE feature, and
  directly optimized. Although the three directions are pairwise 50-81 degrees apart,
  a push at or below the state's own norm along any of them flips most confabulations
  into refusal openings dominated by the "As..." family. Forcing the opener without
  touching the hidden state yields the same one-in-three judged-refusal rate as any
  push. The opposite push collapses refusals mostly into confabulations, yet leaves
  originally correct answers intact, so the direction gates the opening speech act
  rather than the knowledge behind it. Confabulations do not resolve into a
  concentrated feature in a dictionary or a token signature on Qwen3-1.7B. Scoring
  every probe on the margin over the strongest of four prompt-feature baselines
  extracts the hidden state's own contribution. Correctness signal is model-dependent
  and portable only for Llama 3.2 3B: 81% of the probe's margin over test majority
  survives transfer between ConfabQA, PopQA, and TriviaQA. Benchmark, judge, code, and
  cached activations are released.
